\section{Design Process Overview}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Problem definition} – Identify the technical, economic, and legal challenges posed by growing satellite debris.
  \item \textbf{Stakeholder analysis} – Map users, regulators, manufacturers, and the public.
  \item \textbf{Concept generation} – Brainstorm mitigation strategies (tracking, removal, debris‑avoidance algorithms, regulatory frameworks).
  \item \textbf{Concept selection} – Rank ideas by cost, feasibility, and impact using a weighted scoring matrix.
  \item \textbf{Detailing} – Flesh out the chosen concept into a functional system description.
  \item \textbf{Mock‑up creation} – Build a low‑fidelity prototype (digital flowchart, simulation environment) to validate key processes.
  \item \textbf{Evaluation} – Conduct a brief risk and performance review; iterate where needed.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Research – Space‑Law Case: Iridium 33–Kosmos 2251 Collision (2009)}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Span of dates involved} – Collision on September 15, 2009; FCC investigation ran from 2009 through 2010.
  \item \textbf{Countries and companies involved} – United States (Iridium LLC, a US‑based satellite operator) and Russia (Roscosmos, the launch‑and‑operations agency).
  \item \textbf{Major influences on the decision} – 
    \begin{itemize}
      \item The 1967 Outer Space Treaty (OST) emphasized state responsibility but left liability gaps.
      \item US federal enforcement priorities at the time.
      \item Political relations between the two nations, which delayed diplomatic resolution.
      \item Technical constraints on tracking and avoidance in the 2009 environment.
    \end{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Potential outcomes if space law did not exist} – 
    \begin{itemize}
      \item No clear liability would have made compensation for damage impossible.
      \item Operators would have little incentive to maintain debris‑removal systems.
      \item A rapid escalation of collisions and further debris generation could have occurred without accountability mechanisms.
      \item International tension might have intensified, risking a complete suspension of civil space activities.
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

\section{Key Decision Justifications}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Regulatory integration} – Building the mitigation system within the existing OST framework reduces the need for a new treaty and speeds implementation.
  \item \textbf{Industry collaboration} – Engaging Iridium, Starlink, and other commercial operators early ensures cost‑sharing and realistic debris‑tracking data.
  \item \textbf{Technological focus} – Prioritizing on‑board collision‑avoidance algorithms over large‑scale debris‑removal reflects current hardware limits and projected lifecycle lengths.
  \item \textbf{Legal clarity} – Explicitly referencing the 2009 case in the design spec helps operators understand the consequences of negligence.
\end{itemize}

\section{Mock‑up Description}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Architecture} – A cloud‑based service that ingests data from ground‑track sensors, satellite telemetry, and crowd‑sourced reports.
  \item \textbf{Process flow} – 
    \begin{enumerate}
      \item Data fusion \; (cleaning, filtering) → 
      \item Threat assessment \; (probability‑based collision detection) → 
      \item Decision engine \; (generate avoidance maneuvers or issue warnings) → 
      \item Execution \; (transmit commands to satellites or generate de‑bris removal tasks).
    \end{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Visualization} – Web dashboard with real‑time orbit plots, risk heat‑maps, and compliance status indicators.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}
The proposed system leverages existing legal precedents (the 2009 Iridium–Kosmos case) to guide mitigation, aligns with current satellite operator capabilities, and provides a scalable, monitorable framework for reducing space‑junk hazards. 

\boxed{\text{Design completed and mock‑up ready for prototype testing}}

